Let the on-screen fps figure actually appear: NO_TRUST_COUNT 200 -> 20 frames.

STK hides its fps readout for NO_TRUST_COUNT drawn frames after any state
change, showing "FPS: -/-/-" until the countdown expires (irr_driver.cpp, the
`no_trust` branch). Upstream's 200 assumes a 60+ fps machine, i.e. about three
seconds.

This port renders SuperTuxKart at roughly 6 fps, so 200 frames is about 34
SECONDS -- longer than an entire demo race. Measured on hardware: with
`show_fps` enabled the overlay drew "FPS: -/-/- - 63 KTris, Ping: 0ms" for the
whole visible race, i.e. the counter was enabled but permanently useless.

The constant is a frame count, so its settle time scales inversely with frame
rate. 20 preserves the intent -- ignore the first few frames after a transition,
when timings are meaningless -- while settling in ~0.3 s at 60 fps and ~3.5 s
here.

Wanted because the owner asked for live performance figures on screen in the
showcase recording.

